      "content": "Hey, i'm working on an embedded C++ project for an automotive ECU and i've run into a major issue. The system has a gradual RAM exhaustion that only shows up after about 4-5 hours of continuous operation. I've checked most of the obvious spots but i can't find the culprit. \n\nHere is a snippet of the main telemetry loop where i suspect the issue might be surfacing:\n\n```cpp\nvoid process_sensor_data(const SensorPacket& packet) {\n    auto* data_buffer = new uint8_t[packet.size];\n    std::memcpy(data_buffer, packet.raw_data, packet.size);\n    \n    // Process the telemetry\n    TelemetryProcessor::update(data_buffer, packet.size);\n    \n    // Log the event\n    Logger::log_event(\"Data processed\", packet.timestamp);\n    \n    // TODO: Cleanup buffer\n}\n\nvoid run_telemetry_loop() {\n    while (system_running) {\n        SensorPacket packet = hardware_interface.receive();\n        if (packet.is_valid()) {\n            process_sensor_data(packet);\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\nCan you give me a systematic way to track down where this memory is leaking? I can't use heavy tools like Valgrind because of the hardware constraints, so i need a more lightweight or architectural approach.",
